hints.content:
Notice the numerator is missing a $1^{\text{st}}$ degree term. Let's add it as $0x$.

$\begin{array}{r}
x\phantom{+4}
\\
x-3|\overline{x^2+0x+4}
\\
\mathllap{-(}\underline{x^2-3x\phantom{+1}\rlap )}
\\
3x+4
\end{array}$

options.explanation:
We start by asking, "what can we multiply by $x-3$ to get $x^2+k$ (no $x$-term) where $k$ is an integer?"

The answer is $x+3$ (remember the difference of squares pattern):

$\blueD{(x-3)(x+3)=x^2-9}$

Now we rewrite the numerator and split the quotient:

$\begin{align}
\dfrac{x^2+4}{x-3}&=\dfrac{(\blueD{x^2-9})+13}{x-3}
\\\\
&=\dfrac{\blueD{x^2-9}}{x-3}+\dfrac{13}{x-3}
\end{align}$

Now we use the fact that $\blueD{(x-3)(x+3)=x^2-9}$ to rewrite the numerator of the first quotient and cancel common factors:

$\begin{align}
&\phantom{=}\dfrac{\blueD{x^2-9}}{x-3}+\dfrac{13}{x-3}
\\\\
&=\dfrac{\cancel{\blueD{(x-3)}}\blueD{(x+3)}}{\cancel{x-3}}+\dfrac{13}{x-3}
\\\\
&=x+3+\dfrac{13}{x-3}
\end{align}$
